VT_TALK("sql/sql-subqueries", {
 "scalar": "A subquery is a query inside brackets inside another query. It lets you answer two step questions in one go. To find products that cost more than the average, the inner query works out the average, and the outer query compares every product with it. When a subquery gives back exactly one value, it is called a scalar subquery, and you can use it anywhere a single value fits, in WHERE, in the select list, or inside a sum. Be careful that it really returns one row. SQLite quietly takes the first row if there are more, while PostgreSQL raises an error. Think of asking a friend for the class average before deciding who scored above it.",
 "in": "A subquery can return a whole column, a list of values, and IN checks whether a value is in that list. Customers with a pending order are customers whose id appears in the list of customer ids from pending orders. NOT IN finds the opposite, such as customers who never ordered. Remember the trap from the NULL chapter, if the list holds even one NULL, NOT IN returns nothing at all, so be careful when the inner column can be empty. Other engines also offer ANY and ALL, for tests like bigger than every value in a list, but SQLite does not have them. Think of a guest list at a wedding gate, IN means your name is on the list.",
 "exists": "A correlated subquery mentions a column from the outer query, so it is asked again for every outer row. EXISTS is true when the inner query finds at least one row, and it does not care what the row holds, which is why people write SELECT one inside it. Watch the animation ask the question once per customer. NOT EXISTS finds customers who never ordered, and unlike NOT IN it is safe when NULLs appear. The second example finds the most expensive product in each category, by comparing each product with the top price of its own category. Engines also stop EXISTS at the first match. Think of a teacher asking each student in turn, did you bring your homework.",
 "derived": "A subquery can also sit in the FROM clause, where it builds a temporary table for the outer query to read. This is called a derived table, and it must have a name. It shines when you need to aggregate first and then work with the aggregate, for example items per customer, then compare each customer with the average. You cannot write AVG of SUM directly, so the derived table does the first step. The example repeats one inner query twice, which works but is hard to read, and that is exactly the problem the WITH clause solves in a later chapter. Think of making a rough draft on a separate sheet of paper before writing the final answer.",
 "levels": "Climbing the ladder again. For a beginner, a subquery is a small question you answer first. At college level, scalar subqueries give one value, IN subqueries give one column, and derived tables give a whole table, while correlated subqueries are asked once per outer row. At masters level, optimizers unnest subqueries into joins, so EXISTS becomes a semi join and NOT EXISTS an anti join. At research level, a 2015 paper by Neumann and Kemper showed how to remove correlation from any nested query, and modern engines build on that. Think of a good cook who reads a recipe with many sub steps and reorganises the kitchen work to finish faster."
});
